for today's classic interview we're  going back to an interview that I  actually mentioned last week and there  could not possibly be better timing for  this given what's going on with net  neutrality back in August of 2014 I  interviewed someone named Crystal High  who was booked on the show under the  pretense of being a sort of you know a  great speaker about the importance of  net neutrality blah blah blah and I  uncovered during the research phase  before the interview that she had  actually been booked by a cable industry  lobbyist also a lobbyist for the  internet service provider industry and I  basically outed her during the interview  it gets sort of awkward at one point but  it is endlessly fascinating we will go  back to my interview with crystal high  about net neutrality from August of 2014  take a look at this I'm joined today by  crystal high who is the founder and  editor-in-chief of politics 365 and  we're here today to talk about net  neutrality Internet freedom crystal I I  have to admit I'm not particularly  optimistic right now about the future of  Internet freedom and when I step back I  see that we have a former cable  executive Tom wheeler working within the  FCC I see that we have astroturf groups  being funded by the cable lobbies posing  as citizens groups meant to sway public  opinion on the matter and when I step  back and think about five years down the  line I can't say I'm particularly  optimistic about having a true free and  open Internet do you agree with me on  that or do you disagree yeah I mean  honestly I think you should be  optimistic because one of the things  that we're finding now around the whole  net neutrality discussion is that  there's a lot of vocal participation by  people on all sides of the issue I'm  generally inclined to believe that you  know there are certain principles that  everybody would agree to right nobody  wants their content block nobody wants  to see their content degraded or slowed  down this whole notion of you know fast  lane slow lanes I think that by and  large people are saying oh no no that  that's not anything we're looking for  unless there are those cases where say  you know you have a  medicine Ave we're talking about public  safety applications and things that  enhance education so I think there is  broad consensus truly around the goals  that we're trying to protect in terms of  preserving an open Internet I think what  we're finding some disagreement and some  frustration now is really around how do  we get there for one but then you know  the argument is also kind of devolved in  my opinion to this nitpicky sort of you  know so who's in your pocket how are you  making money and I think that misses the  point of the actual issue here something  I am generally optimistic that we will  arrive at a solution that is beneficial  to keeping the internet as we like it to  keep it as an engine of a open  expression and a place for commerce and  entrepreneurship and ideas and  innovation but I think it's going to  take some stepping back a little bit  from the table and I kind of thrown some  rhetoric to the side and say hey what is  it that we're really trying to achieve  here and how to best get that done well  let's take it a piece at a time I mean I  completely agree with you that there  definitely are certain basics that the  general consensus seems to be a kind of  on board with and you're absolutely  right about that but let me push back a  little bit about the who's in your  pocket thing kind of being a distraction  and I'll tell you an interesting story  you may find the story interesting a few  months ago I was approached and I was  pitched a guest his name is Everett  Ehrlich he was the former I believe it  was Undersecretary of Commerce for Bill  Clinton and he was pitched to me by the  DCI group and I said this sounds like a  really great guest he was pitched as  like a progressive guy for Internet  freedom so on and so forth and I  interviewed him and during the interview  he said some things that started  striking me kind of like the corporate  talking points that I had been seeing on  right wing websites and I said that's  interesting let me let me take a step  back for a second and I looked into the  DCI group and as it turns out the DCI  group has indeed been retained by  broadband of America they've been  creating these fake citizens groups they  are a top Republican lobbyists they are  they have Verizon as one of their  biggest clients and I got duped right I  book someone without looking into who  was booking them for me and as it turns  out you were booked through the exact  same organization the DCI group so what  is your relationship to that  organization how does this all work yeah  I mean I think the best way to describe  it is that I'm someone who has you know  after I was in law school and even prior  to that not always had an interest in  media and communications has been a  platform that I think is bar none one of  the best that we have to not only  describe you know what it is we want  from our country what it is we want for  ourselves but it's a platform that  enables a lot right from ideas to  commerce anything like that the Internet  is the Internet is a platform right  you're saying the Internet is this  platform yeah because I mean if you  think about media period right it's kind  of the bastion of free expression and  ideas but the Internet overlays some  really interesting nuances and aspects  to everything right because it does make  sure that we have some broader  opportunities for people who might not  be able to get into the media through  traditional routes and so this has been  something a space that I've worked in  and have developed a passion for over  several years now and I think whenever  you have people particularly people of  color who are willing to kind of speak  out on issues that matter to them you  find different groups whether it's DCI  group or whatever you know I mean their  PR firms all across the country that are  working on both sides of the issues no I  understand that get honest laughing  thank you I totally understand that  heart crystal but you know I mean so  you're african-american I'm Hispanic you  know etc but I don't know that that's  really in other words when I step back  and I want to since you brought up that  you don't think who's in whose pocket is  that important of an issue I find it  very interesting that one of the issues  I mentioned which is that there are  these fake consumer groups and so-called  Pro citizens groups that are really  right-wing lobbyists sending people  around that's a real concern to me well  I mean I think that should be a concern  but you also have to be careful to  distinguish what is this so-called  fake group and what a legitimate  organization that have had a long  history of working towards issues of not  only Internet equality but you know  media justice generally and so I think  that you know some of the criticism  we've seen lately has been a bit  worrisome to me because it picks people  who have even before these consultant  firms are astroturf groups and even  coming to existence you have  organizations that for decades for  centuries have been working for you know  in some instances of straight media  justice and other instances it's a  broader civil rights agenda a broader  consumer agenda that when they happen to  speak out on issues of importance to  their can to their constituencies that  there's somehow you know a problem or  like oh why are these groups talking out  on these issues I mean actually that's  the part that's troubling to me it's  assuming that an organization is an  astroturf group or that an organization  doesn't have a legitimate interest in  the matter at hand just because of who  might have pitched them or how you heard  of a group or who supports an event  that's what I think is problematic to me  that may be the case but when we step  back there's no question right that DCI  has been retained by a cable lobbyist  and has Verizon as a client if you're  getting paid by a cable lobbyist and one  of the big companies that has a stake in  this wouldn't any reasonable person  question whether that is going to be an  organization that has the best interest  of consumers in mind or of their clients  right I mean there's a fiduciary  responsibility to the client you would  you would not deny that well I would  also like to think though that if people  are out front talking about issues if  they're willing to put their reputations  and their lives kind of on the line  because you know they are being front  and center I would like to think that  these are things that these people  actually believe and they're not just  being published you know I don't exactly  but unfortunately crystal the problem  with that argument is the same argument  we hear when we find out that  pharmaceuticals pay doctors to talk  about certain medications and when you  ask the doctor the doctor says you know  what I wouldn't talk about this  medication if I didn't think it was  worthwhile anyway and then the obvious  question  then why does somebody need to pay you  right it's the exact same issue with the  front groups around net neutrality which  is if these are the positions the  individuals hold anyway why does someone  feel the need to pay them we'll see I  don't think and you know correct me if  I'm wrong maybe you have information and  insight that I don't have but from what  I've seen a lot of the organizations  that are being called to the table  aren't being paid to talk about an issue  they might get an event sponsorship for  a program that's totally unrelated so if  you start talking about you know kind of  corporate social responsibility of the  operation investing in the organization  that kind of represent people who are  ultimately consumers of whatever said  corporation does that to me is something  that's fundamentally different than  saying hey I am paying you oh I totally  agree I agree with you which is kind of  why I was going to the question of at  the beginning of the interview to kind  of circle back which and I think this is  fascinating by the way what you said was  you don't think that the issue of who's  in whose pocket is that important and  you now said it may not necessarily be  that people are getting paid what is the  relationship here right because we know  that DCI is paid by broadband of America  the lobbyists are individuals like you  and Everett Ehrlich are you paid by DCI  I think you have to you know really  consider what it is you're suggesting  you're asking there you know like if  people are working on different issues  there could be a say consulting  arrangement that's separate and apart  from whatever it is people are  advocating for so I think you just have  to look at that issue so in other words  what you're saying is DC I may be paying  you as a consultant but they're not  paying you for the media appearances or  being a spokesperson so to speak for the  point of view that their clients espouse  right I mean you know everybody has some  sort of different kind of business  arrangement that they're looking to do  and did people have different ways that  they make their money that they are you  know however they are I happen to be a  lawyer I also happen to be  um you know editor-in-chief of an online  magazine I'm not exactly sure where all  F does I think he's a researcher or  something but you know everybody has a  different relationship so I think you'd  have to go through line by line and  figure out okay so what are you doing  what are you doing but if the fact of  the matter remains that people are  saying things that they legitimately  believe their views that they hold and  have held even prior to or before any  sort of relationship with whatever  consulting firm whatever group it is  you're trying to call to test I think  that that person's individual views is  that's what should be considered as kind  of fun and for most the most important  issues I don't disagree with you but I  do go back to this question of if that  were the case then why why are people  getting paid at all right in the doctor  medication example I think is the one  that stands in any case we've been  speaking with crystal high founder and  editor-in-chief of politic 365 really  helping us to shed some light on what is  and is not as she indicated going on  behind the scenes when it comes to the  net neutrality discussion crystal really  a pleasure having you on thanks so much  for have to do that

